# yamllint config for the workflow files in .github/workflows/ and the
# dependabot config beside them. The workflows are where every merge gate in
# this repo is defined, so a YAML error in one is a gate that stops running
# rather than a gate that fails, and no other tool in the tree parses them:
# gofmt, staticcheck, vet, biome, ruff, mypy and black all read something else.
# dependabot.yml moves the pins those gates rest on, and GitHub reads a broken
# one the same silent way: no update is offered and the tree ages on pins
# nobody proposed moving.
#
# Extends the default rule set (syntax, indentation, key duplication, trailing
# spaces, colons, brackets), so the finding is a defect and not a taste. The
# three deviations from it, and why:
#
#   line-length 200: the longest line is an actions/checkout pin, `uses:` plus
#     a 40-character SHA plus the release tag. The default 80 would fail every
#     pinned action line, and unwrapping a SHA is not a defect to fix.
#   comments-indentation: the pinned `uses:` lines carry a trailing `# v7.0.1`.
#     That comment names the tag the SHA resolves to, so it stays.
#   truthy: `on:` is a GitHub Actions key and a YAML 1.1 boolean, so every
#     workflow trips the default rule on its first line. Checking values would
#     also reject `enabled: false` nowhere and `no-await-in-loops: off` nowhere
#     else. Disabled rather than narrowed, because the key cannot be rewritten
#     without breaking the workflow.
#   document-start: a workflow is not a YAML document anyone concatenates or
#     includes, and `---` as its first line is not a GitHub Actions convention.
#     Its absence says nothing about the file being wrong.
extends: default

rules:
  line-length: { max: 200 }
  comments-indentation: disable
  document-start: disable
  truthy: disable
